        "body": "The spotlight has found Toronto early, and it isn't looking away. <strong>Kawhi Leonard's arrival has reset the ceiling</strong> - Bobby Webster is talking like a man who expects contention, not a patience project, and that is a sharper edge than this franchise has carried in years. The Raptors enter camp with a roster that looks mostly settled, but the real work is in the cracks: who locks down the last rotation minutes, how RJ Barrett and Jakob Poeltl fit the new shape, and whether Leonard's presence changes the pecking order from the first drill. <strong>Saturday's preseason opener against Miami</strong> is the first live look at all of it, a dress rehearsal before the regular-season curtain rises against Chicago on Oct. 21. <strong>That's when the noise stops and the hierarchy starts to speak.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated September 27, 2026"
